Consumer purchasing decisions are typically dominated by upfront price rather than lifecycle costs. By making durability visible and quantifiable, manufacturers can shift consumer perception toward long-term value. Transparency in product longevity creates competitive differentiation in markets increasingly concerned with resource efficiency.

Longevity labelling supports informed consumer choice, enabling buyers to calculate true cost of ownership. This information asymmetry correction helps establish market conditions where durable products compete fairly against cheaper alternatives. Studies show consumers will pay 15-25% premiums for quantified durability when clearly communicated.